\title{Continuous Images Preserve Connectedness}
\taxon{Theorem}
\import{macros}
\p{
    If #{X} is [connected](def-005Q) and #{f:X\to Y} is continuous, then the
    image #{f(X)}, with its subspace topology, is connected.
}
\proof{
    First regard #{f} as a map #{\bar f:X\to f(X)}. This corestriction is
    continuous: if #{U=f(X)\cap O} is open in the subspace #{f(X)}, with #{O}
    open in #{Y}, then
    ##{
        \bar f^{-1}(U)=f^{-1}(O),
    }
    which is open in #{X}.

    Suppose #{f(X)=U\cup V} were a separation. Continuity of #{\bar f} makes
    #{\bar f^{-1}(U)} and #{\bar f^{-1}(V)} open in #{X}. They are disjoint
    and cover #{X}; they are also nonempty because #{\bar f} is surjective.
    They would therefore form a separation of #{X}, contradicting
    connectedness.
}
